<Layout
	title="Reciprocal Ratios | The Concepts Adda"
	description="Learn reciprocal trigonometric ratios: cosec, sec and cot with clear definitions, formulas and examples for Class 10 Mathematics."
>

	<main class="lesson-page">

		<div class="lesson-container">

			<a href="/trigonometry" class="back-link">
				<span class="back-arrow">←</span>
				<span>Back to Trigonometry</span>
			</a>

			<section class="lesson-header">

				<div class="lesson-number">
					03
				</div>

				<div class="lesson-header-copy">

					<div class="lesson-label">
						LESSON 03 · CLASS 10 MATHEMATICS
					</div>

					<h1>
						Reciprocal <span>Ratios</span>
					</h1>

					<p>
						Understand cosec, sec and cot by learning
						how they are related to sin, cos and tan.
					</p>

				</div>

			</section>

			<section class="content-layout">

				<article class="lesson-content">

					<div class="topic-label">
						01 · THE BASICS
					</div>

					<h2>
						What are Reciprocal Ratios?
					</h2>

					<p>
						Reciprocal trigonometric ratios are obtained by
						taking the reciprocal of the three basic ratios:
						<strong>sin θ</strong>,
						<strong>cos θ</strong>
						and <strong>tan θ</strong>.
					</p>

					<p>
						This gives us three more important ratios:
						<strong>cosec θ</strong>,
						<strong>sec θ</strong>
						and <strong>cot θ</strong>.
					</p>

					<div class="idea-box">

						<div class="idea-icon">
							✓
						</div>

						<div class="idea-content">

							<strong>Key Idea</strong>

							<p>
								A reciprocal ratio is simply the reciprocal
								of another trigonometric ratio.
							</p>

						</div>

					</div>

					<div class="topic-label second-label">
						02 · THREE RECIPROCAL RATIOS
					</div>

					<h2>
						The three important ratios
					</h2>

					<p>
						There are three reciprocal trigonometric ratios:
						<strong>cosec θ</strong>,
						<strong>sec θ</strong>
						and <strong>cot θ</strong>.
					</p>

					<div class="ratio-grid">

						<div class="ratio-card">

							<div class="ratio-number">
								01
							</div>

							<div class="ratio-name">
								cosec θ
							</div>

							<div class="ratio-equals">
								=
							</div>

							<div class="math-fraction ratio-fraction">
								<span class="fraction-top">1</span>
								<span class="fraction-bottom">sin θ</span>
							</div>

							<div class="ratio-description">
								Reciprocal of <strong>sin θ</strong>
							</div>

						</div>

						<div class="ratio-card">

							<div class="ratio-number">
								02
							</div>

							<div class="ratio-name">
								sec θ
							</div>

							<div class="ratio-equals">
								=
							</div>

							<div class="math-fraction ratio-fraction">
								<span class="fraction-top">1</span>
								<span class="fraction-bottom">cos θ</span>
							</div>

							<div class="ratio-description">
								Reciprocal of <strong>cos θ</strong>
							</div>

						</div>

						<div class="ratio-card">

							<div class="ratio-number">
								03
							</div>

							<div class="ratio-name">
								cot θ
							</div>

							<div class="ratio-equals">
								=
							</div>

							<div class="math-fraction ratio-fraction">
								<span class="fraction-top">1</span>
								<span class="fraction-bottom">tan θ</span>
							</div>

							<div class="ratio-description">
								Reciprocal of <strong>tan θ</strong>
							</div>

						</div>

					</div>

					<div class="topic-label second-label">
						03 · FORMULA CONNECTION
					</div>

					<h2>
						Remember the pairs
					</h2>

					<p>
						Each basic trigonometric ratio has one reciprocal
						ratio. Memorise these three pairs carefully.
					</p>

					<div class="formula-table">

						<div class="formula-row formula-header">

							<div>
								Basic Ratio
							</div>

							<div>
								Reciprocal Ratio
							</div>

							<div>
								Relationship
							</div>

						</div>

						<div class="formula-row">

							<div>
								<strong>sin θ</strong>
							</div>

							<div>
								<strong>cosec θ</strong>
							</div>

							<div class="formula-text">
								<span>cosec θ =</span>

								<span class="inline-fraction">
									<span>1</span>
									<span>sin θ</span>
								</span>
							</div>

						</div>

						<div class="formula-row">

							<div>
								<strong>cos θ</strong>
							</div>

							<div>
								<strong>sec θ</strong>
							</div>

							<div class="formula-text">
								<span>sec θ =</span>

								<span class="inline-fraction">
									<span>1</span>
									<span>cos θ</span>
								</span>
							</div>

						</div>

						<div class="formula-row">

							<div>
								<strong>tan θ</strong>
							</div>

							<div>
								<strong>cot θ</strong>
							</div>

							<div class="formula-text">
								<span>cot θ =</span>

								<span class="inline-fraction">
									<span>1</span>
									<span>tan θ</span>
								</span>
							</div>

						</div>

					</div>

					<div class="topic-label second-label">
						04 · TRIANGLE RELATION
					</div>

					<h2>
						Using the sides of a triangle
					</h2>

					<p>
						Just like sin, cos and tan, reciprocal ratios can
						also be expressed using the sides of a right-angled
						triangle.
					</p>

					<div class="triangle-card">

						<div class="triangle-visual">

							<svg
								viewBox="0 0 600 370"
								role="img"
								aria-label="Right angled triangle showing perpendicular, base, hypotenuse and theta"
							>

								<!-- Triangle -->

								<polygon
									points="105,300 105,75 505,300"
									fill="#eef4ff"
									stroke="#14213d"
									stroke-width="5"
									stroke-linejoin="round"
								/>

								<!-- Right angle -->

								<polyline
									points="105,268 137,268 137,300"
									fill="none"
									stroke="#2563eb"
									stroke-width="5"
								/>

								<!-- Perpendicular label -->

								<text
									x="55"
									y="190"
									text-anchor="middle"
									class="svg-label"
									transform="rotate(-90 55 190)"
								>
									Perpendicular
								</text>

								<!-- Base label -->

								<text
									x="305"
									y="340"
									text-anchor="middle"
									class="svg-label"
								>
									Base
								</text>

								<!-- Hypotenuse label -->

								<text
									x="330"
									y="165"
									text-anchor="middle"
									class="svg-label"
									transform="rotate(29 330 165)"
								>
									Hypotenuse
								</text>

								<!--
									THETA ANGLE

									The bottom-right vertex is (505,300).

									The angle is between:
									• the horizontal base
									• the diagonal hypotenuse

									The arc is kept close to the vertex.
								-->

								<path
									d="M 445 300 A 60 60 0 0 1 453 271"
									fill="none"
									stroke="#f4c430"
									stroke-width="4"
									stroke-linecap="round"
								/>

								<!--
									THETA LABEL

									Placed clearly along the angle bisector
									outside the arc for maximum readability.
								-->

								<text
									x="425"
									y="278"
									text-anchor="middle"
									dominant-baseline="middle"
									class="angle-label"
								>
									θ
								</text>

							</svg>

						</div>

						<div class="triangle-info">

							<div class="side-info">

								<div class="side-dot blue-dot"></div>

								<div>

									<strong>
										cosec θ
									</strong>

									<span>
										Hypotenuse ÷ Perpendicular
